{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Short-horizon (t+1 to t+7) bias: forecasts are slightly too low in downtrends and early recoveries. Apply a small upward level shift (~+0.2 to +0.5 €/t) when the last 5–10 days show falling prices followed by stabilization (i.e., slope turning from negative to flat).",
    "Turning-point lag: the model tends to recognize reversals 1–3 days late (keeps projecting the prior drift). Heuristic: if the last 3 days are up after a multi-day decline, tilt the next 3–5 forecast days upward (reduce projected decline / add +0.3 to +0.8 €/t depending on recent volatility).",
    "Medium-horizon (t+10 to t+25) underestimation during rallies: when spot rebounds strongly (e.g., move from low 60s back to mid/high 60s), forecasts often cap the upside and stay below realized levels by ~1–3 €/t. Add an upside extension: if 10-day momentum > +2 €/t, raise days t+10..t+25 by ~+1.0 to +2.0 €/t.",
    "Late-horizon (t+20 to t+30) spike handling: forecasts sometimes overshoot downward (projecting drops to ~60) while outcomes revert upward (mid/high 60s). Impose mean-reversion anchoring: constrain the last-third of the path toward a rolling 20–30d mean (e.g., blend 50% forecast with 50% 20d mean) unless trend strength is extreme.",
    "Volatility damping around sharp jumps: after large up-moves in the true series, the model often underreacts (too smooth), then remains too low for several days. If a >2 €/t single-day jump occurs in history, increase the next 5–10 forecast days by ~+0.5 to +1.5 €/t and reduce any immediate projected pullback magnitude by ~25–40%.",
    "Amplitude bias: forecast paths are generally too low-variance versus realized outcomes (miss higher highs during rebounds). Apply a regime-based scaling: in high-volatility regimes (e.g., 10d realized vol above recent median), expand deviations from the mean by ~10–20%."
  ]
}
